    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Five dynasties of Turkic and Afghan origin ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> for 320 years, from 1206 to 1526.</li><li>It repelled repeated Mongol invasions and fostered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indo-islamic-culture">Indo-Islamic culture</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qutb-minar">Qutb Minar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amir">Amir</a> Khusrau.</li><li>Overreach, provincial breakaways and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sack-of-delhi">sack of Delhi</a> weakened it before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babur">Babur</a> won at Panipat in 1526.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Moroccan traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qazi">qazi</a>, or judge, at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> from 1334.</p></aside>
    </section>

