    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-of-ghor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor.jpg" alt="Muhammad of Ghor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27izz_al-Din_Muhammad._AH_599-602_AD_1171-1206.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Delhi Sultanate emerged from the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghurid-empire">Ghurid Empire</a> after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> in 1206. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</a>, a Turkic slave-general who had governed the Ghurid conquests in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> since the victory at Tarain in 1192, assumed independent power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lahore">Lahore</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluk</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk-dynasty-delhi">Slave Dynasty</a>. The new state was fragile, contested by rival Ghurid successors such as Taj al-Din Yildiz of Ghazni and Nasir al-Din Qabacha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#multan">Multan</a>, by resistant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> rulers, and by the instability of an administration built on personal loyalty rather than institutions. Consolidation accelerated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iltutmish">Iltutmish</a>, who seized the throne in 1211, defeated Yildiz at the third Battle of Tarain in 1216, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tanka">tanka</a> as the standard coin, and organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqta</a> system of revenue assignments. The critical moment of external legitimation came in 1229, when the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mustansir Billah sent Iltutmish a formal diploma of investiture (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manshur">manshur</a>), recognising him as sultan of the lands he ruled in India. This investiture ended the sultanate's symbolic dependence on Central Asian overlords and established Delhi as an independent imperial centre, providing the ideological foundation on which later dynasties built their authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#delhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi.jpg" alt="Delhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jama_Masjid_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcin Białek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate ruled one of the most populous and diverse territories of the medieval world, governing a predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> agrarian population through a Muslim ruling elite of Turkic, Afghan and Persian origin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqta</a> system, under which the right to collect revenue from designated territories was assigned to military officers in lieu of salary, formed the backbone of both economic organisation and military mobilisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> (r. 1296–1316) intervened radically in this system, paying much of his army in cash, raising the land tax to half the produce in the core provinces, and imposing fixed prices enforced through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#market-reforms-of-alauddin-khalji">state-controlled markets</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> for grain, cloth, horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> and slaves — the most ambitious experiment in centralised economic management in medieval Indian history. It worked during his reign, keeping grain prices stable even in times of drought, but lapsed soon after his death in 1316 because it depended on his personal enforcement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a>'s token currency experiment of around 1329–1330, which introduced brass and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#token-currency-of-muhammad-bin-tughlaq">copper tokens</a> to be accepted at the value of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, led to mass counterfeiting and financial chaos and had to be withdrawn, illustrating how the sultanate's economic innovations repeatedly outran its administrative capacity to sustain them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#first-battle-of-panipat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat.jpg" alt="First Battle of Panipat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_battle_of_Panipat_and_the_death_of_Sultan_Ibr%C4%81h%C4%ABm,_the_last_of_the_L%C5%8Dd%C4%AB_Sultans_of_Delhi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ẓahīr ud-Dīn Muḥammad Bābur (1483-1530) It contains 30 mostly full-page miniatures in fine Mughal style by at least t..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Military power in the Delhi Sultanate was predominantly cavalry-based, drawing on successive waves of Turkic and Afghan martial traditions, supplemented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephants</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> built the largest standing army the sultanate ever fielded — 475,000 horsemen according to the later chronicler Firishta, a figure that is certainly inflated — paid directly by the state rather than through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqta</a> assignments. His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> led the most spectacular campaigns in the sultanate's history, compelling the submission of the Kakatiyas at Warangal (1310), extracting enormous tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a> and plundering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madurai">Madurai</a> (1311), bringing nearly every major southern Indian power into tributary submission. Equally significant was the sultanate's repeated success against Mongol invasions: Alauddin's forces repelled a series of large-scale Chagatai incursions between 1297 and 1306, including the great invasion under Qutlugh Khwaja that reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> in 1299, sparing northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> the devastation suffered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The army's final failure came at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> on 21 April 1526, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>'s much larger army, reportedly some 100,000 strong, was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babur">Babur</a>'s combination of field guns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matchlock">matchlocks</a> behind a barrier of linked carts and the flanking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tulughma">tulughma</a> manoeuvre — among the first decisive uses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gunpowder">gunpowder</a> artillery in north Indian warfare.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#balban"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balban.jpg" alt="Balban" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#balban">Balban</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Ghayas-ud-Din_Balban.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Five successive dynasties — the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluk</a>, Khalji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a>, Sayyid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> — governed the sultanate across 320 years, each with distinct administrative emphases but all facing the same tension between centralising royal authority and the power of the military nobility. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balban">Balban</a>, who became sultan in 1266 after some two decades as the dominant minister under Nasir ud-Din Mahmud, confronted this tension most directly by breaking the power of the Forty (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chihalgani">Chihalgani</a>), the circle of Turkic nobles who had dominated the court, introducing Persian court ceremonial to raise the ruler far above the nobility, and building an extensive network of spies. Islamic law provided the formal legal framework, administered through the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qazi">qazis</a>, while Persian served as the language of administration and high culture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transfer-of-capital-to-daulatabad">Transfer of capital to Daulatabad</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> in 1327, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a>'s forced relocation of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a>'s elite population, intended to improve control of the southern provinces, instead devastated the capital, was soon reversed, and accelerated provincial rebellion. Firuz Shah Tughlaq (r. 1351–1388) later made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqtas</a> hereditary, further strengthening the nobility. The Lodi dynasty's failure to manage its Afghan nobles, who expected to be treated as peers rather than subjects, ultimately produced the invitations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babur">Babur</a> that ended the sultanate from within as much as from without.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#qutb-minar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-minar.jpg" alt="Qutb Minar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#qutb-minar">Qutb Minar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qutb_Minar_2022.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wasir kasab, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Delhi Sultanate was the principal conduit through which Islamic legal, architectural and administrative culture became deeply rooted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Persian literary and artistic traditions fused with Indian forms to produce a distinctive Indo-Islamic synthesis, visible in architecture from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qutb-minar">Qutb Minar complex</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a>'s Alai Darwaza to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> tombs, in the poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amir">Amir</a> Khusrau, and in the emergence of the Hindavi vernacular that would later give rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#urdu">Urdu</a>. The sultanate's successful resistance to Mongol invasion preserved northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> as a functioning centre of civilisation during the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries, when much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a> was devastated, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> a refuge for scholars, artisans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sufism">Sufis</a> fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a>; the Chishti Sufi order, led in Delhi by Nizamuddin Auliya, flourished under its rule. The Moroccan scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>'s service as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qazi">qazi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a>'s court from 1334 reflects the sultanate's place in the wider world of Islamic scholarship and its ambition to project cosmopolitan legitimacy. The fragmentation of the subcontinent into regional sultanates after the Tughlaq decline permanently altered South Asian political geography, creating the multi-state landscape that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> would later struggle to overcome.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sack-of-delhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sack-of-delhi.jpg" alt="Sack of Delhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sack-of-delhi">Sack of Delhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadir_Shah_at_the_sack_of_Delhi_-_Battle_scene_with_Nader_Shah_on_horseback,_possibly_by_Muhammad_Ali_ibn_Abd_al-Bayg_ibn_Ali_Quli_Jabbadar,_mid-18th_century,_Museum_of_Fine_Arts,_Boston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">possibly by Muhammad Ali ibn Abd al-Bayg ibn Ali Quli Jabbadar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate's disintegration was set in motion by the very expansion that marked its territorial peak. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a>'s overextension across the south generated rebellions faster than they could be suppressed: the Madurai Sultanate broke away in 1335, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> was founded around 1336, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengal">Bengal</a> became independent in 1338, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> was established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> in 1347. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sack-of-delhi">sack of Delhi</a> in December 1398 delivered a catastrophic physical blow: tens of thousands were killed, by some accounts many more, many of the city's artisans were carried off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samarkand">Samarkand</a>, and the treasury was emptied. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sayyid-dynasty">Sayyid dynasty</a> that followed (1414–1451) ruled as nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timurid-empire">Timurids</a> over a much-reduced territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> recovery under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bahlul-lodi">Bahlul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a> was genuine but limited, never regaining the Deccan or Bengal, though Sikandar founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#agra">Agra</a> as a new capital in 1504. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>'s alienation of his own Afghan nobility proved fatal, as Daulat Khan Lodi, his governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#punjab">Punjab</a>, and his uncle Alam Khan invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babur">Babur</a> to invade, ensuring that the sultanate was destroyed by its own internal contradictions as much as by external force.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Delhi Sultanate's enduring significance lies in its creation of the Indo-Islamic cultural and administrative synthesis that shaped South Asian civilisation through the centuries of Muslim-ruled states, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, that followed it.</em></p>

